# ── Visual style presets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# GENERATED by `python3 docs/design/palette_gen.py --emit-presets`. Do not edit
# a hex here; change the theme in `docs/design/palette_gen.py` and re-emit.
# `backend/tests/test_theme_layering.py` fails on any drift.
#
# ONE palette, since 2026-08-06. There were eight, one per industry, and the
# arrangement had a cost nobody had measured: the PAPER carried the industry
# hue, so whichever semantic role shared that hue stopped being visible. Five
# of the seven light themes had one — cedar-grove's success sat 4 degrees off
# its own page, harbour-calm's info 9, vintage-press's warning 3. A green
# theme could not show "saved".
#
# So the paper and the ink are anchored and constant, the four semantics are
# constant, and a studio gets one knob: the accent hue, taken from its own
# logo and re-solved (see `accent_hue_from` — the hue is kept, the lightness
# and saturation are the product's). Twenty-eight of the forty-three tokens
# are no longer variables.
#
# The default accent is a deep bronze analogous to the paper rather than the
# violet that maximises distance from the four status hues. That is a decision
# taken against the arithmetic: warm paper with a warm accent reads as one
# room, and a violet reads as a brand colour on a beige page. It is only safe
# because a semantic role is never a solid fill — see `Design_Constraints.md`
# section 1.1 — and because the accent's own tinted chip is solved deeper than
# any status chip, which the generator asserts.
#
#     python3 docs/design/palette_gen.py            # verify
#     python3 docs/design/palette_gen.py --table    # inspect

